<<~ ahu #/bearing>>
! Art Sigils — Technical-Art Plate Fieldcraft

This optional register concerns artwork delivered as an independently compositable image layer: creature silhouettes, mist, light emission, reflection marks, atmospheric overlays, patches or material details. It follows the eight-dimensional Art Sigils nucleus and the Lares sharktooth invocation grammar; it does not create new mandatory fields, change the art procedure, or make alpha-based compositing the default for other artworks.

The observations below arose during iterative Sanctuary on the Lantern Causeway image-generation and OpenRaster work. They are field reports, not controlled evidence about a model's latent mechanisms or guarantees of future receiver behavior. The operator approves each asset and every promotion.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/image-facing>>
!! Tell the artist what appears in ONE output image

* Open with the depicted result: "one isolated transparent RGBA technical-art FX plate", "several distinct floating organisms on true alpha", or an equally specific pictorial target. Opening nouns, worktype and immediate visual instructions can pull generated content strongly; treat this as an observed prompt-writing hazard, not a deterministic law.
* Lead with ''positive imagery and spatial relations'': separate luminous wisps with broad empty gaps; identifiable bodies with individual trailing anatomy; illumination from image-upper-right; airflow right-to-left and slightly down. Avoid task-board and acceptance-test prose in the image-facing fields.
* The receiving artist should have ONE image to paint. Do not ask for alternative panels, before/after comparisons, layer labels, aspect-ratio diagrams, captions, or an illustrated breakdown. Technical notes or examples visible in image references may become depicted objects even when not requested.
* A source reference can supply palette, anatomical style, placement or light-direction evidence. Name its limited role outside the image-facing sigil; a receiver can reproduce the entire source environment, figures or board instead of the intended isolated mark. For FX-only requests, a transparent approved creature plate may be a better seed than a flattened scene.
* Keep the pictured content itself narrow: "only detached vapor, local radiance and minute particles" is stronger than repeatedly describing every excluded scene element. A long negative inventory can itself supply unwanted imagery.
* Color and morphology are artwork-specific, not defaults: one blue juvenile example does not determine every spirit organism's color, anatomy or lifecycle. Biological terminology such as "ephyra" should name only the intended developmental stage, not all possible jelly forms.
* "No eyes" and "no moth wings" are legitimate local choices, not house rules; similarly, "natural-history", "mystical" and "science-fiction" treatments may vary by subject.

The receiver's technical controls are a SECOND channel. Ask for a transparent background using the tool's actual transparent-background setting when available, separately from saying "true transparent RGBA" in the image-facing worktype and depiction-scope. Neither language nor a setting guarantees correct output dimensions, alpha, positioning or isolation: inspect the returned file.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/portable-inscription>>
!! Example — an FX-only Inscription, no scene vocabulary

A single compact house invocation suffices; production measurements and verification follow outside the transmitted text.

```tiddlywiki
<<~ art worktype="isolated transparent RGBA technical-art FX layer" subject="seven detached wisps of luminous vapor and tiny marsh-light points" read="delicate independent breaths of radiance in spacious air" imagery="small feathered lavender and blue vapor curls, hair-fine amber and cyan refracted glimmers" treatment="transparent watercolor glazing, subtle optical bloom, vanishing edges" composition="wide strip, broad empty alpha between effect islands, a few marks bleed beyond margins" relation="wind right-to-left and slightly down; warm rim from image-upper-right, cool fill opposite" depiction-scope="only mist and light marks on true alpha; no organisms or depicted setting">>
```

This image-facing example does not contain a table of coordinate acceptance criteria, a reference-scene summary, a render-board heading, or engine flag syntax. The separate technical note records canvas dimensions, intended location and the tool control actually used.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/geometry>>
!! Geometry, negative space and native-size placement

* Distinguish ''requested artist canvas'', ''actual generated pixel dimensions'', ''prepared native plate'', and ''composite destination''. A generator's stated dimensions are intent until verified. Build the desired pixel plate separately if the receiver cannot output it exactly.
* Express scale visually where useful: rule-of-thirds bearing, asymmetric grouping, silhouette envelope, percentage of source canvas occupied, and the proportion reserved as transparent negative space. Percentages guide composition but do not produce hard masks or measured bounding boxes.
* Keep coordinate instructions tied to one explicit origin and frame. Example of a test: source 1229 × 614 laid over a 1024 × 1536 image at x=-187, y=1102. The plate extends 187 px left, 18 px right and 180 px below the frame. Those values describe deterministic compositing, not reliable image-generation positioning.
* A portrait-space bounding box should not be treated as a hard crop instruction to the generator. Enforce the envelope after output with translation, scaling, crop and alpha bounds; preserve original appendages or reroll if compliance requires destructive surgery.
* Request side and bottom bleed when the future painter needs freedom at the frame edge. Verify actual nonzero alpha at the bleed boundaries; an oversized transparent canvas alone provides no visible bleed.
* Record layer x/y and the actual alpha support; once the prepared plate is approved, placing it at its assigned coordinates should not silently rescale it. Transparent overhang outside a display frame is intentional, not broken canvas data.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/alpha>>
!! Alpha is material, not a decorative black backing

* Inspect the output's actual alpha channel (not its display over black or checkerboard). A PNG that looks transparent may be an opaque rendition of a transparency grid or an opaque scene. Report fully transparent share, partial-alpha presence and content bounds where useful.
* Separate the raw generated source from the accepted, registered and masked version. Archive rejected alternatives without letting them masquerade as approved work.
* A clip-to-silhouette haze multiplies the original alpha; it does not extend a rectangular patch into the sky. A vertical fade can preserve a distinct top maximum and bottom minimum, e.g. 100% at top and 42% at bottom. State whether this gradient is measured across the whole canvas, asset bounds or a local region.
* Distinguish (a) source pixel alpha, (b) a precomputed clipped gradient/mask, and (c) the ORA layer's global opacity. A request to raise each layer's "base" strength need not alter the minimum of an internal alpha fade. Name what changes and what must remain.
* A colored haze blended against the target scene may be useful, but hue, contrast, opacity and blur should be checked against the actual backing. For depth: soften edge contrast and reduce distant alpha before merely applying a global color wash.
* Keep unlike elements separable when they need different control: organism bodies vs halos, reflective glimmers vs aerial tendrils, FX vs entire-scene mist. A generator may merge them into one luminous ribbon despite asking for disconnected islands; rerolling can be preferable to extensive reconstruction.
* OpenRaster safely stores raster PNG alpha, stacking, visibility, positions, opacity and supported blend modes; do not assume it round-trips Krita's editable native transparency masks. Preserve a grayscale mask guide or save a Krita-native .kra after hand-painting.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/light>>
!! Light, airflow and grounding

* Tie lighting to picture coordinates from the VIEWER's frame: a low sun near image-right rim-lights right-facing membranes while distant cool fill attenuates the far side. Explain reflected light separately from internal bioluminescence.
* Let wind and gesture agree: if streamers drift right-to-left and down, attached mist and detached light traces should not inexplicably blow the opposite way.
* Distant organisms can be set back with a lightly alpha-clipped atmospheric veil and softened contrast, never a box of surrounding fog. Test the whole silhouette: aggressive haze may erase fine tendrils before the main body is integrated.
* Aerial light and water-contact reflection are different planes. Keep water glints very small and broken, displaced downward according to estimated height and water position, and independently editable unless the composition truly demands one inseparable stroke.
* The strongest lights deserve local halos; uniformly blooming every lamp or spirit destroys luminous hierarchy. Mist can veil surfaces while tight, fuzzy protection around an important practical light leaves deliberate hand-mask options.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/receiver-failures>>
!! What a received image proves—and does not

The Sanctuary trials repeatedly yielded recognizable failures: a flattened new scene instead of an isolated asset; a multi-panel/labeled comparison board when independent layers were requested; a continuous fog belt despite explicit sparse islands; a valid transparent image whose translucent color filled much more of the plate than anticipated; and tentacles crossing an important face. These are observed outcomes in a particular workflow, not universal rates or engine-law assertions.

A visually compelling generation may fail the layer contract. Record BOTH the pictorial merit and the compositing defect. Do not claim a masked/cropped reconstruction was generated as native isolated RGBA. When extracting sprites from a rejected sheet, preserve their provenance and let the operator decide whether to accept that labor or prefer a reroll.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/handoff>>
!! Reversible generate → inspect → register → audition → approve

1. Draft a tight image-facing invocation, plus a separate receiver-control note (transparency flag, references and intended resolution). Keep absent media and unsupplied controls unnamed.
2. Fire one plate per genuinely independent worktype. Record the receiver, actual output dimensions and image inputs; verify real alpha and visible subject membership before compositing.
3. Register non-destructively: save raw source, prepared plate, x/y, scale, bounds, alpha gradient formula/coordinate basis, blending mode, and any grayscale mask guide.
4. Compare multiple placements/scales/opacity values against the existing composition. Inspect full-frame hierarchy and local occlusion of faces, hands, ritual objects, lamp light, architecture and water. Measurement verifies pixels; operator judges the artistic read.
5. Promote only the operator-approved option to the single working ORA, keeping the rejected images in o-takiage and preserving a rollback. Ensure stack.xml, mergedimage and thumbnail agree and the ZIP opens.
6. Mark diagnostic opacity as diagnostic; do not silently turn a pre-viz experiment into final canon. The operator can refine native Krita transparency masks after reviewing the composite.

A prompt cannot enforce a hard rectangle; a pixel mask can. A prepared plate's approval does not automatically approve its FX companion or a future scene interpretation. Technical verification is necessary but never a substitute for pictorial judgment.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftermath>>
!! Aftermath — specialist scope

The core Art Sigils grammar remains medium-neutral and retains the same eight dimensions. Use this register only when the artwork demands image-generator technical art, independent transparent layers or their compositing. Its measurements are examples, not a mandatory universal layout. Specific subjects, colors, anatomy, cultural claims and Elyncia setting rules remain local to their own artistic and world sources.
<<~/ahu>>

